{"id":"69070af7-e665-4258-a44f-e1683099fd87","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885807,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    mins = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\\n    caps = [seg['capacity'] for seg in segments]\\n    imps = [seg['importance'] for seg in segments]\\n    total_min = sum(mins)\\n    total_cap = sum(caps)\\n    if budget < total_min or budget > total_cap:\\n        raise ValueError('Budget out of range')\\n    alloc = mins[:]\\n    allocated = total_min\\n    while allocated < budget:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_ratio = -1.0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if alloc[i] < caps[i]:\\n                ratio = imps[i] / (alloc[i] + 1)\\n                if ratio > best_ratio:\\n                    best_ratio = ratio\\n                    best_idx = i\\n        # If best_idx remains -1, something went wrong (should not happen given capacity check)\\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\\n        allocated += 1\\n    by_name = {}\\n    for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\\n        by_name[seg['name']] = alloc[i]\\n    return {\\n        'allocations': alloc,\\n        'by_name': by_name,\\n        'total': allocated\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that implements the described algorithm. The case has segments with name, importance, minimum, capacity. Budget is an integer. We need to allocate exactly budget tokens. Start each segment at its minimum. Then while total allocated < budget, repeatedly give one token to the segment that maximizes importance/(current allocated+1) (since next allocated position is current+1? Actually \"importance divided by its next allocated position\" means if current allocation is x, next allocation will be at position x+1 (1-indexed), so ratio = importance / (x+1). We break ties by original segment order (presumably the order they appear in segments list). Also we need to reject budgets below sum of minimums or above total capacity sum. Return object with allocations (list of integers in same order as segments), by_name (dict mapping segment name to allocation), total (sum of allocations). Also \"dynamicKeysFrom\" indicates by_name keys come from segments[*].name, so we just use names as keys. Output must match schema exactly.\n\nWe'll implement solve accordingly. Use standard library only. No mutation of case.\n\nEdge cases: budget exactly sum of minimums, or exactly total capacity. Also note that we must ensure we don't exceed capacity per segment. While loop: while allocated < budget: for each segment compute candidate if not at capacity, choose best ratio. If none can be increased (all at capacity) but budget not reached? That would be invalid, but we already check budget <= total capacity; so it's consistent.\n\nImplementation details: deepcopy? Not needed, we only read case. We'll build allocations list from segments dict.\n\nReturn dict with keys allocations, by_name, total.\n\nNow produce JSON response with single key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a string, properly escaped. The entire response must be a JSON object, no markdown.\n\nNeed to ensure code is valid Python, uses no external imports beyond builtins. Use standard json module for serialization? The function returns a dict and the evaluator will serialize? Actually output contract says return a JSON value. So we return a Python dict/list etc. No need to import json inside solve.\n\nWrite solve function.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1469,"completion_tokens":787,"total_tokens":2256,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":459},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":317},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod08